"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers the first two key points well. It frames closure risk around break-even AUM against fixed and variable costs, which captures why a small fund can lose money at a reasonable fee. It also addresses who bears the shortfall (waivers, caps, subsidies, the sponsor's ability and willingness to absorb losses) and correctly says AUM alone does not determine closure. The third key point is only partly covered. It treats seed quality and persistence generically ('temporary or affiliated seed may overstate durable demand'), but it never says that market-maker seed is temporary inventory that can be redeemed, as opposed to more lasting investor seed. It also does not say that subsidized seed investors can leave. It mentions market makers only in a liquidity context. It omits entirely the series-trust board's power to replace the adviser or liquidate the fund. These are substantive omissions from a central key point, so the grade is partial.\"}","ttft_ms":2585,"type":"result","duration_ms":5249,"uuid":"5efdd9d4-7a30-4f79-bfc7-f33fd7fad78c","ttft_stream_ms":562,"time_to_request_ms":68,"first_content_frame_ms":562,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
